In article <1115@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu> arrom@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu (Ken Arromdee) writes:

>How do you keep players from colluding (i.e., one starts as a Valkyrie,
>	another a Samurai, another a Wizard; 2 kill themselves off after
>	handing all equipment to the other, so one player has a katana,
>	and an armor class of -1, and an 18 strength immediately?  There are
>	other forms of collusion, i.e. one player stays, the other keeps sending
>	in new characters whom the first one kills for lots of experience;
>	one player starts as a tourist and gives all his gold to the other,
>	etc...)
I would add an ownership attribute to each of the objects in the game.  This
attribute could attach an object to a particular player in the game; no other
player would be allowed to pick up that object.  Some objects in the dungeon
would allow anyone to hold them; this would allow some amount of sharing
without allowing one character to get too strong too fast.  The initial
inventory would be attached to the character in this way; he could drop an
item (like his katana) but no-one else would be able to pick it up  ("Sorry,
but that katana is for Bilbo").  This has the additional benefit of allowing
slower players to have a chance at picking up some items on a level before a
faster (or a player who has been on the level longer) could clear it out.

